{"title":"Orbit Climbing","sub":"A climbing gym that could become too popular","type":"Investment / capacity","industry":"Leisure","geo":"Canada","diff":3,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Ramp-up · peak capacity · payback","prompt":"Orbit wants to open an indoor climbing gym in a converted warehouse in Calgary. The fit-out (walls, flooring, safety systems) costs $2.2M. The plan assumes membership grows to 1,200 people by year three. The investors' hurdle is a payback within five years. Should they go ahead?","clarify":[{"q":"Revenue?","a":"Memberships at $95 a month, plus day passes worth about $0.45M a year."},{"q":"Costs?","a":"Rent $0.38M, staff $0.42M, other $0.15M a year. Variable costs (card fees, chalk, cleaning) about 5% of revenue."},{"q":"Ramp-up?","a":"Members: 600 in year one, 1,000 in year two, 1,200 in year three."},{"q":"Safety?","a":"The gym can safely hold 120 climbers at once."}],"structure":{"root":"Does the gym pay back within five years?","branches":[{"head":"Revenue","items":["Members × monthly fee","Day passes","Ramp-up over three years"]},{"head":"Costs","items":["Rent, staff, other (fixed)","Variable costs"]},{"head":"Capacity","items":["How many climbers are there at peak?","Does crowding cap membership?"]}]},"hypothesis":"Gyms have high fixed costs, so the ramp-up matters. But the bigger risk may be the evening peak: if 1,200 members all want to climb after work, the gym may be too full to sell that many memberships.","questions":[{"q":"Calculate operating profit for years one to three and the payback.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"396.533pt\" height=\"163.444pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 396.533 163.444\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M 0 163.444 L 396.533 163.444 L 396.533 0 L 0 0 L 0 163.444 z\n\" style=\"fil